WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:06.070 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  i could feel myself starting to sweat  and a pit forming at the base of my  stomach  sure signs i was nervous  kind of like right now  i was 22 years old and i had was sitting  in my first ever apartment  all by myself  staring at my computer screen in the  dark i was about to send my entire life  savings to something called vanguard  i had just graduated from college with  an economics degree and i knew investing  was important but i had no idea how to  start  i must have spent hours doom scrolling  on vanguard's website trying to learn  all of this new vocabulary  there were etfs and mutual funds in  indices  i had so many questions  what do i invest in  how exactly does investing work  and why does it matter  i must have called vanguard's customer  success line like 10 different times  with my long list of questions  and the men who answered the phone did  their best to answer my questions about  the what and the how  but i still didn't know why i felt like  i was sending my entire life savings  into the ether  i didn't understand the investing  language i felt unconfident in my  knowledge and mostly  i felt like i didn't belong  it took calling my 90 year old grandma  before i had the nerve to open my  account  and my grandma grew up with the great  depression with very little in her life  and started investing later on  and i asked her all of the same  questions i asked the men at vanguard  and  just picturing her answering the phone  in her kitchen started to release that  knot in my stomach  she explained to me why investing  mattered  she met me patiently and seeing her  confidence made me feel confident too  she said julianne if you save and invest  as much as you can now you can live a  life of freedom  just start small  and take the first step  so i did  i opened an account in a vanguard mutual  fund and as i started to feel more  comfortable i started to participate  more  i invested in individual company stocks  i even voted at shareholder meetings and  over time  i built my investing confidence  i was reminded of my grandma's advice  three years ago when a few of my guy  friends told me to start investing in  cryptocurrency  and i felt like crypto was important but  i could feel that familiar knot forming  at the bottom of my stomach  i had so many questions how does crypto  work what is it exactly and why does it  matter  i asked my guy friends to explain it to  me but their answers felt like alphabet  soups of nfts and dows and btcs  i joined a few online forums to try to  get my questions answered there and i  was met with either silence or outright  contempt from the male dominated groups  everything felt unfamiliar and everyone  was either anonymous or a guy  in one instance i was blocked from a  community after spending 30 minutes  trying to figure out the right person to  ask a question to  i must have asked it to the wrong person  and if it felt like i was sending my  life savings into the ether with  vanguard  in crypto i was trying to buy something  called ethereum  despite having 10 years of experience in  finance  i didn't understand the crypto language  i felt unconfident in my knowledge  and mostly  i really felt like i didn't belong  this time i couldn't call my grandma for  support but luckily my friend brooke  explained to me why crypto mattered and  brooke seeing brook feel confident made  me feel confident too  brook said julianne cryptocurrency gives  equal access to financial services to  anyone across the world  and crypto is just like any other  currency we use in our day-to-day life  except crypto uses a network of  computers called the blockchain and not  banks to verify track and manage  transactions  so just like my grandma's advice i  started small  i opened a crypto account i purchased a  few currencies and as i started to feel  more comfortable i started to  participate more  i invested in currencies where i could  vote on future projects and  over time  i built my crypto confidence  i've now worked in crypto for nearly  three years and in that time i've spoken  to hundreds of women from all over the  world who want to participate in the  crypto economy  and we share a similar story daniela  from columbia says my husband works in  crypto and he tried to explain it to me  but  i couldn't catch up with all the new  words he was saying  i wanted a how-to guide for how to get  started  mira from new york says  i tried to join online communities but  everyone just assumes you're a guy  i would join and people would say hey  bro  let me just say  if you are a woman and you have tried to  get started in crypto and maybe you felt  like you didn't belong  it's not you  it's crypto  today just 15 of investors in bitcoin  the most popular global cryptocurrency  are women  and many crypto companies report 90 or  more of their users or men  the language we hear the products we use  the communities we see  reflect current users  today mostly men  and women want to participate in the  crypto economy in fact a recent study  suggests globally more women are  interested in buying cryptocurrency than  they are public company stocks  and in the u.s  more women have expressed interest in  cryptocurrency than men  it turns out the world of crypto could  learn a thing or two from my grandma  because the thing that myself and  daniela and mira all had in common  a trusted friend who helped us take the  first step  who explained to us why crypto mattered  who met whom and seeing their confidence  made us feel confident too  now i can't be your grandma but i can be  your trusted friend  to design a crypto economy for women we  need to do three things  first  women need to see her we need to explain  why crypto matters  let's talk more about equal access to  financial services and use fewer  acronyms  the unintended consequence of using  acronyms  we leave people behind  if you're explaining crypto to a friend  take the extra moment to spell out what  you're saying  second we need to design products and  communities with women in mind  if you're building in crypto make sure  women test your products  and if you're designing online  communities and forums consider reducing  anonymity in order to increase safety  for all of your users including and  especially women  and third  and this is mostly for my crypto curious  women in the room  take the first step  when women take the first step today  when we open a crypto account when we  buy a cryptocurrency when we use a  crypto product  we get a say in future products and  services  companies will need to start building  for us  and when crypto gets easier for women it  gets easier for all of us  the crypto economy will become more  accessible and it will be because of the  women in this room and the women around  the world and some pretty incredible men  and people everywhere who say  we want our future economy to look and  feel more like all of us  because we need all of our voices heard  in this new economy  it will take a diverse set of people  representative of our global community  to build a more equitable economy  tomorrow we will all use crypto to  access our global economy today  today all we need to do  is take my grandma's advice  and take the first step  [Applause]  [Music]

